% -e shows each query's Magic rewrite.  edge is derived by facts alone, so
% it is read completely and evaluated first, under its program heading.  The
% rewrite then lists the adornments and the seeds it generated, and the
% components of the rewritten rules, indented beneath it, before the
% rewritten query's plan.
%
% path(a, X)? calls path with its first column bound.  The recursive rule
% passes the binding along edge, so magic$path$bf is recursive too, and holds
% every node reachable from a.  path(X, d)? binds the second column, and the
% planner puts the recursive call first, where it is called with that same
% column bound; the magic rule that would record the call derives
% magic$path$fb from itself and is dropped, leaving the seed alone.

edge(a, b).
edge(b, c).
edge(c, d).

path(X, Y) :- edge(X, Y).
path(X, Z) :- edge(X, Y), path(Y, Z).

path(a, X)?
path(X, d)?
